and `prawduct-hook test-status` exits 1 on it. The failing test is test_a_link_opened_with_a_modifier_is_left_to_the_browser. This bundle rewrote it in Chunk 12 (1840a6c3): the work id is now read from `.first` before the Ctrl/Cmd-click, and `wait_for_url` plus `assert` became `expect(...).to_have_url`. Read its failure text (`testament history '<id>' --json`) before deciding anything. If the new form asserts something the page does not do, fix the test or the code; do not weaken the URL claims. If it is a flake, say so in the record with the history that shows it. Then rerun and record the suite. Note that #441 and #442 name other tests, so their known-flaky status does not cover this one.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_navigation.py"], "failed_tests": ["arrt.tests.browser.test_navigation::test_a_link_opened_with_a_modifier_is_left_to_the_browser[chromium]"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "declared_deadlines now silently ignores a deadline kind it does not spell out, where replace(**chosen) used to raise", "recommendation": "Instance; the class is type-only rewrites that swap a loud failure for a silent default. The Chunk 10 type fix in `library/sources/loading.py` swapped `replace(base, **chosen)` for four hand-named keywords (`chosen.get(kind, base.<kind>)`). It justifies this with the comment \"`DEADLINE_KINDS` is exactly these four\", and nothing checks that: no test references DEADLINE_KINDS. Before, a kind that was in DEADLINE_KINDS but not spelled in the call raised TypeError the first time a plugin declared it. Now it loads, the declared deadline passes validation, and the plugin runs at the default. Wave 2b's Chunk 32 owns this file. Learnings: \"When a comment justifies code by a constraint, or names what a guard excludes, check the constraint\" and \"When a comment names a symbol as the source of truth for a set, parametrise the test over that symbol\". Fix: add a test parametrised over DEADLINE_KINDS. It should assert that each kind is a float field of `Deadlines` and that a deadline a plugin declares for it shows up on the returned `Deadlines`. Or build the keywords from DEADLINE_KINDS in a form mypy accepts.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/sources/loading.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "FeedPublisher is also every collaborator's wall and theme lookup, so reads that touch no feed depend on the publisher", "recommendation": "Class. The split put the existence-checking lookups (`get_wall`, `get_theme`, `require_hanging`, `current_draft`) on `FeedPublisher`. Its module docstring says it is 'everything that decides a wall's feed and writes it', but the other four collaborators make 33 of these calls through `self._feeds`: walls.py 10 `get_wall` and 2 `get_theme`, themes.py 7 `get_theme` and 5 `get_wall`, reports.py 6 `get_wall`, scenes.py 3 `get_wall` and 2 `current_draft`. Some of those callers never publish. `Reports.last_showing`, `largest_screen` and `published_manifest_v2` need a FeedPublisher only to ask whether a wall exists. The same lookup and its 'No wall with id \u2026' sentence are also written out again in `clients.py` (lines 851-853). Two things here compound. The plan's tradeoff says wave 2b's Chunks 38 and 45 will call the collaborators directly so the facade can shrink, and wiring HTTP and MCP to those collaborators would then pull in the publisher for plain reads. And 2b's Chunk 37 ('not found is 404') has to change exactly these lookups, which now sit inside the feed publisher and are copied in clients.py. Fix: give the require-lookups one owner below every collaborator, for example module-level `require_wall(store, wall_id)` and `require_theme(store, theme_id)` over `ProgrammingStore`. The publisher, the four collaborators and `clients.py` would all call it, and `FeedPublisher` would keep only what builds, stages and publishes. To bound it, search `programming/` for `_feeds.get_wall`, `_feeds.get_theme`, `_feeds.require_hanging`, `_feeds.current_draft` and the strings 'No wall with id' and 'No theme with id'.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/feeds.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/reports.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/walls.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/themes.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/scenes.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/clients.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The newer-release guard compares release numbers, not schemas, so a rollback that crosses no migration is now refused, and the way back it names may not exist", "recommendation": "Instance. `refuse_a_newer_release` (arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py) refuses whenever the recorded release's (major, minor, patch) is greater than this one's. That includes a downgrade across no schema change at all, such as rolling a bad v0.12.1 back to v0.12.0, which is the commonest rollback there is. `take_pre_migration_snapshot` copies only when `pending_drops` finds something, and the change record says plainly that no copy is taken for `catalogue_opened`. So in that case the refusal's own remedy, \"restore the copy taken before the newer release migrated the file\", names a file that was never written. The operator's only ways back are a backup generation, which loses everything since it was taken, or hand-editing the row. The plan's DECISION ('a release opening a file whose recorded release is newer refuses to start') records why the guard exists, but it does not weigh this cost. The records still promise the opposite in three places: architecture.md about line 1312 ('A rollback that crosses no migration is the previous image alone'), architecture.md about line 1806, and operational-spec.md \u00a7 Routine Operations, the Rollback row ('A rollback that crosses no migration is still the old two-step'). For any release that carries the guard, those sentences are now false. A fix needs one of two things. (a) Key the refusal on something that changes only when the schema does, such as a schema generation the migrations bump, so a schema-neutral downgrade opens. (b) Record the cost as a vetoable decision, give the refusal a way back that exists when no copy was taken (name the newest backup generation and what it loses), and correct the three sentences. A test should open a file recorded by a newer patch release with an identical schema and assert whichever behaviour is chosen.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The refusal message sends the operator to operational-spec's Rollback row, which omits the journal step the restore test proved necessary", "recommendation": "Instance. `NewerRelease`'s message in arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py ends '(operational-spec.md \u00a7 Routine Operations, Rollback)'. That row reads 'git checkout the previous commit, then restore catalogue.sqlite from the copy taken before the migration ran, then restart both'. It has no step to remove `catalogue.sqlite-journal` (or a -wal/-shm) first. This wave's `test_a_restore_that_leaves_the_stale_journal_is_silently_undone` measured what happens without that step: a copy put in beside a hot journal opens as the dead server's last commit, and `integrity_check` says ok. The likeliest time a rollback is needed is right after a failed or killed upgrade, which is exactly when a hot journal is present. An operator who follows the message to that row does the rollback the test shows is silently undone. deploy/README.md's 'The way back across such a migration' and operational-spec \u00a7 Backup and Restore's numbered steps both have the journal step. Point the message at one of those, and make the Rollback row cite the numbered steps rather than restate them (it also still speaks of 'git checkout' and restarting 'both', the Pi-era topology). The row clears the Records Pass's 'misleads into action' bar.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The real-Player DECISION said the pool tests the shipped pair on every server change; as built nothing does, and no amendment records why", "recommendation": "Instance. The plan's DECISION reads 'the real-Player test lives in Arrt, on the pool ... | Arrt's pool tests the shipped pair on every server change'. As built, `player_e2e` is deselected by arrt/pyproject.toml's addopts, so the pool's curation suite never selects it, and docs/testing.md says 'Nothing runs it unasked: not the default suites, not tests.yml'. Only image.yml runs it, on a pull request into main. Chunk 08's built note lists four departures, and this is not one of them. The reason recorded for deselecting it (it needs the network to GitHub, plus git and uv) does not apply to the pool, whose runners 'reach GitHub and carry both' (docs/testing.md, measured there at 210 s cold and 81 s warm). The consequence: a server change that breaks the pair in a way the fixtures miss merges into develop green, and is found at the release pull request a week of commits later, blamed on the whole release. Either select it on the pool (for example a curation-suite selection that adds `-m player_e2e` for arrt/tests/system/test_a_real_player.py), or amend the DECISION under \u00a7 Decisions made mid-build with the reason (cost, the cold install) and the detection lag it accepts.", "files": ["arrt/pyproject.toml", ".testament.toml", "docs/testing.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-audit-wave-2.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A pull request merged during a pool outage leaves no machine-readable mark that its tree never had a browser verdict, and the plan's stated backstop does not exist", "recommendation": "Instance. In `mode=fallback` the required check 'Lint, then every suite' passes. The only record that the browser suite never ran is the job summary and a `::warning::` annotation. Two records give two different backstops, and neither has an enforcer. The plan's Goals tradeoff says 'a pull request merges on governance, contract and curation evidence alone. The next pool run on `develop` is the browser's verdict'. But no workflow runs on a push to develop: tests.yml is `pull_request` and `workflow_dispatch` only, and observability-strategy says 'a push to `main` runs nothing'. tests.yml's header, docs/testing.md and the summary row instead say to run the browser suite on the pool before merging, which is a manual step behind a green required check. The repository's agent workflow merges once CI is green. So a browser regression merged in an outage surfaces on the next unrelated pull request's pool run, blamed on that pull request. Ruling 4 says an outage must not block a merge, and it does not say the gap may go unrecorded. Give the fallback a mark that does not block: for example a second, non-required commit status or check ('browser suite: not run, pool unavailable') set on the head SHA, which the merge UI shows and a script can query. Then correct the plan's tradeoff sentence to name whichever backstop is real.", "files": [".github/workflows/tests.yml", ".github/scripts/pool_or_fallback.py", "docs/testing.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-audit-wave-2.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The migration chain's release list grows by hand, and nothing adds a new release's dump, so 'every past release' goes stale at the next schema change", "recommendation": "Class: the RELEASES tuple in arrt/tools/old_catalogue.py and arrt/tests/fixtures/catalogues/releases.json. The tool's docstring says 'A new release whose schema changed gets its dump by adding its tag to RELEASES'. Nothing in the release process, operational-spec or docs/testing.md asks for that, and no test checks the list against releases. The only check, `test_there_are_dumps_of_at_least_two_releases_and_each_is_on_disk`, holds a floor of two. project-preferences' norm row says 'a catalogue every past release left opens on today's shape', and that becomes false the first time a release that changed the schema ships without a dump. The very next one does: this wave adds `catalogue_opened`. The defect this test just found (v0.1.0's `tv_display` rows) is the class that would then go unseen. To close it: a governance-suite test that fails when the newest release with a schema change (for example the version arrt/pyproject.toml names, once it differs from releases.json's newest key, at a release commit) has no dump, or a step in the release procedure that the release check reads. Per the core rule 'When you write or cite 'exactly one', 'always' or 'never', state what the store or guard actually enforces', until then the norm row should say 'every release in releases.json'.", "files": ["arrt/tools/old_catalogue.py", "arrt/tests/system/test_migration_chain.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 02:361", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `.prawduct/backlog.md` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 02:366", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `.prawduct/backlog.md` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 02:400", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `.prawduct/backlog.md` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 03:527", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `manifest.v2/valid/media-on-another-host.json` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-12"}], "counts": {"blocking": 5, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The catalogue_opened record says 0.11.0 for code that is not v0.11.0", "recommendation": "`this_release()` reads the installed package version, and `arrt/pyproject.toml` still says 0.11.0. The change record calls v0.11.0 the release that does not carry the record. So until the release bump, every open by this code (develop, a branch image) writes release '0.11.0', and an operator reading the row before a rollback reads a release that never wrote it. This does no harm if the bump always comes before an image is deployed. If that ordering is not already enforced, make sure the release step bumps the version first.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "DisplaySettings is defined in the facade module, which every collaborator below it imports under TYPE_CHECKING to dodge the cycle", "recommendation": "The facade docstring says 'each collaborator calls only those above it in that list', but feeds.py, walls.py, scenes.py and reports.py (and clients.py) all import `DisplaySettings` from `display.py`, the module that composes them. The cycle is legal only because each import sits under `if TYPE_CHECKING:`. The first runtime use of it in a collaborator (an isinstance check, a default instance) would break the import. Moving `DisplaySettings` into a small module of its own, re-exported from display.py, would make the import order the same as the stated layering. Instance.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The facade keeps `_library` and `_settings` it never reads, and `_store` that only tests read", "recommendation": "`DisplayService.__init__` stores all three, but no facade method uses them. `_store` is read by test_deleting_a_wall.py and test_display_facade.py, and nothing reads `_library` or `_settings`. Drop the two unread attributes, or keep them deliberately for the identity checks in test_display_facade.py. Instance; no edit is wanted unless one is already happening.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "With the ratchet empty, the type gate's ratchet comparison, its sort check and `_to_toml` guard a list that another test forbids", "recommendation": "`test_no_module_is_excused_from_the_type_check` refuses any `ignore_errors` override. That leaves `test_the_ratchet_lists_exactly_the_modules_that_fail` as a full mypy run whose only possible failure is 'mypy reports errors', which CI's lint step already checks, at a cost the file itself measures (22 s warm, minutes cold) on every governance run. `test_the_ratchet_is_sorted_and_names_each_module_once` can no longer fail. The docstring records the choice, so this is noted rather than rated: a simpler gate is 'no ignore_errors override' plus one plain `uv run mypy` exit check, without the TOML re-serialiser.", "files": ["tests/preferences/test_type_gate.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`record_the_open(completed=\u2026)` is two different statements behind one flag", "recommendation": "With `completed=True` the function ignores its `release` and `now` arguments and runs an UPDATE. With `completed=False` it runs an upsert. Two named functions, for example `record_the_open_begun(release, now)` and `record_the_open_completed()`, would say which write each migration-list entry makes without reading the body. Instance; cosmetic.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "vocabulary.json's `_about` says its words are 'as Walls says it on a wall's badge', but Walls says 'No Player' for `unassigned`", "recommendation": "The plan deliberately chose 'No Player yet' (Chunk 03), and the shared-values table defers the server's quote to 2b's Chunk 31. So the divergence is planned, and `unassigned` is a state the server reports, which a Player's settings page does not show. Today, though, walls.js BADGES and STATE_WORDS, and information-architecture.md \u00a7 Walls, say 'No Player', and the `_about` sentence is fixed in the tag once `contract-v1.2.0` is cut. Making the sentence relational ('the words Walls is to say') before the tag would keep the release from asserting a present-tense agreement that is false for one of the seven states.", "files": ["contract/vocabulary.json"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The real-Player pin is a commit on Arrt Player's integration branch, which a squash-merge and branch deletion can leave unfetchable", "recommendation": "arrt/tests/system/player-ref.txt pins a0a0f690\u2026 on arrt-player's fix/audit-wave-2. If that branch is squash-merged and deleted, `git fetch --depth 1 <repo> <sha>` can stop finding the commit once GitHub prunes it. The release-PR `player` job would then fail at install (by name, so it would not be silent). Moving the pin to the Player's merged or tagged commit when one exists, as the file already says to, closes it.", "files": ["arrt/tests/system/player-ref.txt"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: the oracle set covered", "recommendation": "Rated against the three bars: build-plan-audit-wave-2.md (frontmatter, Goals, decisions, chunks 04/06/08, mid-build notes), changes/audit-wave-2.md (whole), operational-spec.md (\u00a7 Backup and Restore, \u00a7 Routine Operations), operator-verification.md (this wave's three entries), owner-rulings.md (the 2026-11-19 section), player-contract.md (this wave's additions), project-preferences.md (this wave's rows), architecture.md (the two rollback sentences), observability-strategy.md (the CI and drift rows). Two records cleared a bar, and both are folded into findings above because each has a subject-file cause: the Rollback row (finding 2) and the plan's two false premises (findi